
########################## SCANNER ##########################
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
#                                                           #
# Bruker PET insert Si 198                                  #
#                                                           #
# Axial FOV: 150 mm                                         #
# Air system envelope inner diameter: 113.8 mm              #
# Detector material: LYSO                                   #
# Crystal dimensions: trapezoidal (50 mm x 50 mm exit       #
#       face, 48 mm x 48 mm entrance face, 10 mm thickness) #
# Number of detectors per ring: 8                           #
# Number of detector rings: 3                               #
# Reconstruction voxel size: set in the analysis            #
#                                                           #
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

# SPATIAL RESOLUTION (NEMA NU 4-2008 section 3), GATE 8.2.
# A Na-22 point source in a 10 mm acrylic cube, at the 16 positions of Kuntner et al. 2025
# (Table 3, Quad86 coil): radial offsets 0-35 mm at the axial centre and at 1/4 of the axial FOV.
# The Quad86 coil and the mouse or rat cradle are loaded from coil_bed_real/. Coil and cradles are
# cut at z = +-80 mm (beyond the detectors) to save time. The cradle moves with the source along z
# only; the radial offsets move the source alone, upwards (+y), above the cradle.
# Detector: 8 heads x 3 rings of monolithic trapezoidal LYSO blocks, ring pitch 52 mm, crystal
# entrance faces at r = 58.5 mm (117 mm ring diameter). I fitted the 52 mm ring pitch to the axial
# sensitivity profile measured by Kuntner et al. 2025; it is not taken from a Bruker drawing.
# The detector, physics and digitizer are those of the sensitivity and count-rate models, with two
# differences: the energy resolution is 15.2 % (measured on this scanner at the activity of the point
# source) instead of 18 %, and the energy window is 30 % (357.7-664.3 keV).
# GATE stores the energy-weighted interaction centroid in the crystal. There are no 0.48 mm pixels,
# SiPM position errors or DOI response in this macro: spres_analysis.py adds the detector position
# errors and the photon acollinearity after the simulation.
# A Ga-68 point source is prepared in the SOURCE section (commented out; spres_run.py --isotope Ga68).
# The intrinsic Lu-176 source is part of the macro. All results in the paper were run with it switched
# off (spres_run.py --no-lu176).

#    exit face
# ---------------- 
# \			  /   Trapezoidal shape of the crystal
#  --------------  
#   entrance face



######################## RUN SETTINGS #######################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The only values that change between positions. spres_run.py writes one copy of this file per
# position and changes only these lines (and, with --bed rat, the cradle macro in the CRADLE
# section). Run directly, this file simulates the 10 mm radial position at the axial centre with
# the mouse cradle.
# Positions (Kuntner et al. 2025, Table 3, Quad86): radial offset 0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 mm
# at the axial centre (z = 0) and at 1/4 of the axial FOV (z = 37.5 mm).
# No comments on the alias lines themselves: an alias takes the rest of its line as its value.
#   sourceX, sourceY, sourceZ : source position in mm. The cradle follows sourceZ only (the bed
#                               moves along z, never radially). The radial offset is along +y,
#                               upwards, away from the cradle (along x the cube would run into the
#                               side walls of the cradle). sourceZ 0 = axial centre,
#                               37.5 = 1/4 axial FOV
#   activity                  : Bq (measurement: 140 kBq)
#   duration                  : s (measurement: 60 s per position)
#   seed                      : random seed, different for every position
#   output                    : ROOT file name without .root (spres_run.py: SpRes_<bed>_z<z>_r<r>)
#   cradleY                   : cradle height in mm, set by spres_run.py --bed: mouse 7.045, rat 19.740.
#                               At these heights the 10 mm cube centred on the axis rests on the
#                               cradle floor with a 0.01 mm gap, as in the sensitivity model.

/control/alias sourceX 0
/control/alias sourceY 10
/control/alias sourceZ 0
/control/alias activity 140000
/control/alias duration 60
/control/alias seed 123456789
/control/alias output output/SpRes_mouse_z0_r10
/control/alias cradleY 7.045



########################## MATERIALS ########################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The materials database in the folder Material.

/gate/geometry/setMaterialDatabase Material/GateMaterials.db



########################## VERBOSITY ########################
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# How much GATE prints while it runs (0 = minimal, higher numbers = more detail).

/gate/verbose Physic    5
/gate/verbose Cuts      0
/gate/verbose SD        0
/gate/verbose Actions   0
/gate/verbose Actor     0
/gate/verbose Step      0
/gate/verbose Error                               1	# show simulation errors
/gate/verbose Warning                             1	# show navigation, physics and sorter warnings
/gate/verbose Output    0
/gate/verbose Volume    0
/gate/verbose Image     0
/gate/verbose Geometry  0
/gate/verbose Core      0

/run/verbose 0
/event/verbose 0 
/tracking/verbose 0



########################## WORLD ########################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

# The world volume, in which the scanner and all other volumes are placed. Think of it as the
# scanner room: any space that is large enough to hold the scanner.

/gate/world/setMaterial Air
/gate/world/geometry/setXLength 100 cm
/gate/world/geometry/setYLength 100 cm
/gate/world/geometry/setZLength 100 cm
/gate/world/vis/setVisible 0



########################## PET MODEL ########################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

### PET SYSTEM ###
# The PET system is a daughter of the world. I use the GATE system PETscanner because it accepts
# other crystal shapes, such as the trapezoidal blocks here. The system name must be written exactly
# as in the GATE documentation.

/gate/world/daughters/name                 PETscanner	# system name from the GATE documentation
/gate/world/daughters/insert               cylinder	# a cylindrical envelope
/gate/PETscanner/setMaterial               Air		# 
/gate/PETscanner/geometry/setRmin                 56.9 mm	# inner radius of the air envelope (113.8 mm bore); the front pad lies inside it
/gate/PETscanner/geometry/setRmax                 99 mm	# outer radius of the air envelope
/gate/PETscanner/geometry/setHeight               155 mm	# length of the air envelope; the crystals reach z = +-77 mm
/gate/PETscanner/placement/setTranslation  0 0 0 cm	# placed at the centre of the world
/gate/PETscanner/vis/setColor              red		# colour in the viewer
/gate/PETscanner/vis/setVisible            0			# 0 = not shown, 1 = shown
#/gate/PETscanner/vis/forceWireframe


### HEAD ###
# A head holds one detector sector: its modules, copper sheets and front pad. It is a daughter of
# PETscanner and must be at least as large as the parts it holds.
# The heads are 45 degree air sectors that do not overlap. Their local origin is the scanner
# origin, so the translations of the parts below are their real positions in the scanner.

/gate/PETscanner/daughters/name      head
/gate/PETscanner/daughters/insert    cylinder
/gate/head/setMaterial              Air
/gate/head/geometry/setRmin          56.9 mm
/gate/head/geometry/setRmax          75 mm
/gate/head/geometry/setHeight        155 mm
/gate/head/geometry/setPhiStart      67.5 deg
/gate/head/geometry/setDeltaPhi      45 deg
/gate/head/placement/setTranslation  0 0 0 mm
/gate/head/vis/setVisible            0			# 0 = not shown, 1 = shown
#/gate/head/vis/forceWireframe


### MODULE ###
# The module holds one crystal. It has the same trapezoidal shape as the crystal: a 50 x 50 x 10 mm
# box would cross the sector boundary near the entrance face.

/gate/head/daughters/name                module
/gate/head/daughters/insert              trap
/gate/module/setMaterial                Air
/gate/module/geometry/setDx1             25 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDx2             25 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDx3             24 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDx4             24 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDy1             25 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDy2             24 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setDz              5 mm
/gate/module/geometry/setTheta           0 deg
/gate/module/geometry/setPhi             0 deg
/gate/module/geometry/setAlp1            0 deg
/gate/module/geometry/setAlp2            0 deg
/gate/module/placement/setTranslation    0 63.5 0 mm	# 117 mm ring diameter: crystal entrance faces at r = 58.5 mm
/gate/module/placement/setRotationAxis   1 0 0	# rotate around the x axis only
/gate/module/placement/setRotationAngle  90 deg	# rotate by 90 degrees
/gate/module/vis/setVisible              0		# 0 = not shown, 1 = shown
/gate/module/vis/forceWireframe


### CRYSTAL ###
# The scintillation block that detects the annihilation photons: one monolithic LYSO block with a
# trapezoidal shape (dimensions in the header above).
# The material LYSO does not create intrinsic Lu-176 decays by itself. The Lu-176 activity is a
# separate source (Intrinsic Activity Source below).

/gate/module/daughters/name             crystal	# the name is free in the PETscanner system
/gate/module/daughters/insert           trap		# trapezoid: unlike a box, it has 6 degrees of freedom
/gate/crystal/setMaterial               LYSO 	# scintillator material from GateMaterials.db
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx1           25 mm	# X1 at the exit face (half of the 50 mm block width)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx2           25 mm	# X2 at the exit face (half of the 50 mm block width)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx3           24 mm	# X3 at the entrance face (half of the 48 mm block width)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDx4           24 mm	# X4 at the entrance face (half of the 48 mm block width)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDy1           25 mm	# Y1 at the exit face (half of the 50 mm block width)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDy2           24 mm	# Y2 at the entrance face (half of the 48 mm block width)
/gate/crystal/geometry/setDz            5 mm		# half of the 10 mm block thickness
/gate/crystal/geometry/setTheta         0 deg	
/gate/crystal/geometry/setPhi           0 deg
/gate/crystal/geometry/setAlp1          0 deg	
/gate/crystal/geometry/setAlp2          0 deg	
/gate/crystal/placement/setTranslation  0 0 0 mm	# at the centre of the module
/gate/crystal/vis/setColor              white	# colour of the LYSO block in the viewer
/gate/crystal/vis/setVisible            1		# 0 = not shown, 1 = shown
/gate/crystal/vis/forceSolid					# draw as a solid


### COPPER SHIELDING ###
# Copper sheet in front of each crystal: the RF shielding of the insert (not a shield against the
# static 9.4 T field). The 0.0351 mm thickness is a modelling assumption, taken from Thiessen et al.
# (a different insert). Like all shielding, it attenuates and scatters some PET photons.

/gate/head/daughters/name                   shielding			# free name
/gate/head/daughters/insert                 box				# a box makes a thin sheet
/gate/shielding/setMaterial                 Copper			# see Thiessen et al https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-9155/61/22/7934
/gate/shielding/geometry/setXLength         48 mm				# X size at the entrance face
/gate/shielding/geometry/setYLength         48 mm				# Y size at the entrance face
/gate/shielding/geometry/setZLength         0.0351 mm			# thickness of the copper sheet, see Thiessen et al https://doi.org/10.1088/0031-9155/61/22/7934
/gate/shielding/placement/setTranslation          0. 57.98145 0. mm	# 0.501 mm gap to the crystal entrance face (r 58.5 mm)
/gate/shielding/placement/setRotationAxis   1 0 0				# rotate around the x axis only, because the module, and so the crystal, is rotated
/gate/shielding/placement/setRotationAngle  90 deg			# rotate by 90 degrees
/gate/shielding/vis/setColor                red				# colour of the sheet in the viewer
/gate/shielding/vis/setVisible              1 				# 0 = not shown, 1 = shown
/gate/shielding/vis/forceSolid							# draw as a solid


### Front Pad ###
# The front pad covers the entrance faces of the detector, so that the RF coil sits on it without
# touching the crystals. I approximate it by flat sheets, one on each of the eight detector heads.

/gate/head/daughters/name                  FrontPad		# free name
/gate/head/daughters/insert                box			
/gate/FrontPad/setMaterial                 Nylon			# the material of the real system
/gate/FrontPad/geometry/setXLength         47.00 mm		# X size; eight copies form the faceted front cover
/gate/FrontPad/geometry/setYLength         154 mm			# length of the pad, 154 mm to match the 52 mm ring pitch
/gate/FrontPad/geometry/setZLength         1 mm			# layer thickness
/gate/FrontPad/placement/setTranslation           0. 57.4629 0. mm	# 0.001 mm gap to the copper sheet
/gate/FrontPad/placement/setRotationAxis   1 0 0			# rotate around the x axis only, because the module, and so the crystal, is rotated
/gate/FrontPad/placement/setRotationAngle  90 deg			# rotate by 90 degrees
/gate/FrontPad/vis/setColor                blue			# colour of the layer in the viewer
/gate/FrontPad/vis/setVisible              1				# 0 = not shown, 1 = shown
/gate/FrontPad/vis/forceSolid							# draw as a solid



########################## VOLUMES REPETITION ########################
# Above, only one crystal, one copper sheet and one front pad are defined. The repeaters below copy
# them to build the whole detector.


### REPEAT CRYSTAL ###
# One copy in x, y and z (the module and head repeaters make the copies).

/gate/crystal/repeaters/insert             cubicArray		# repeat on a grid
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1				# copies along x
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1				# copies along y
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  1				# copies along z
/gate/crystal/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0.0 0 0.0 mm	# no spacing (the module and head repeaters place the copies)


### REPEAT MODULE ###
# One copy in x and y, three along z: the three detector rings (the module is a daughter of the
# head).

/gate/module/repeaters/insert             cubicArray		# repeat on a grid
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1				# copies along x
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1				# copies along y
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  3				# three copies along z
/gate/module/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0. 0. 52 mm		# ring pitch 52 mm: 4 mm gap between rings at the entrance faces, 2 mm at the exit faces


### REPEAT SHIELDING ###
# One copy in x and y, three along z, as the modules (the copper sheet is a daughter of the head).

/gate/shielding/repeaters/insert             cubicArray	# repeat on a grid
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1			# copies along x
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1			# copies along y
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  3			# three copies along z
/gate/shielding/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0. 0. 52 mm	# same 52 mm pitch as the modules, so each copper sheet stays on its crystal


### REPEAT HEAD ###
# Eight heads around the z axis make one ring. Each head holds three modules along z, so this gives
# three rings of eight crystals.

/gate/head/repeaters/insert      ring		# repeat around a ring
/gate/head/ring/setRepeatNumber  8			# 8 heads in the ring
/gate/head/ring/setPoint1        0. 0. 1.	# ring axis along z
/gate/head/ring/setPoint2        0. 0. 0.
/gate/head/ring/enableAutoRotation      # rotate each sector and its contents around the ring


### REPEAT FRONTPAD ###
# One copy in x, y and z. The front pad is a daughter of the head; the ring repeats it eight times.

/gate/FrontPad/repeaters/insert             cubicArray		# repeat on a grid
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberX  1			# copies along x
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberY  1			# copies along y
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatNumberZ  1			# copies along z
/gate/FrontPad/cubicArray/setRepeatVector   0. 0. 0. mm	# no spacing


### ATTACH SYSTEM ###
# The volume levels of the PETscanner system (level1, level2, level3) are attached in the order of
# the volume hierarchy.

/gate/systems/PETscanner/level1/attach  head
/gate/systems/PETscanner/level2/attach  module
/gate/systems/PETscanner/level3/attach  crystal


### ATTACH CRYSTALSD ###
# The crystal is the sensitive detector: the hits are recorded in it (Crystal Sensitive Detector).

/gate/crystal/attachCrystalSD


### DESCRIBE SYSTEM ###

/gate/systems/PETscanner/describe



##################### Coil Configuration ####################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The Quad86 coil, the same as in the sensitivity and count-rate models (coil_bed_real/): two G10
# cylinders on the scanner axis with uniform walls, inner tube r 43.0-44.8 mm (1.8 mm), outer tube
# r 54.69-56.89 mm (2.2 mm), z -80 ... +80 mm, material AC_G10 (1.79 g/cm3). The radii are those of
# the 511 keV attenuation map of this coil. The full, uncut coil models are in full_coil_models/.
# coil_bed_real/Coil3Bed.db (AC_G10, Bed_HDPE) is loaded in addition to Material/GateMaterials.db.
# Keep coil_bed_real/ next to this file.

/gate/geometry/setMaterialDatabase coil_bed_real/Coil3Bed.db
/control/execute coil_bed_real/quad86_coil_cut80_uniform.mac



####################### Magnetic Field ######################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The static field of the 9.4 T MR scanner, along the scanner axis.
# spres_run.py --no-field comments this line out in the job macros (runs without the field).

/gate/geometry/setMagField  0  0  9.4 T



###################### CRADLE (MOUSE OR RAT): MOVES ALONG Z ONLY ####################
#//////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The cradles of the sensitivity model (coil_bed_real/), material Bed_HDPE (polyethylene,
# 0.95 g/cm3), cut to z -80 ... +80 mm:
#   mouse (default): mouse_cradle3mm_cut80_sens.mac, made from the rat cradle by scaling its
#                    cross-section by 0.487, with solid side walls about 3 mm thick; cradleY 7.045 mm
#   rat            : rat_cradle_hdpe_cut80_sens.mac, the rat cradle taken from the attenuation map
#                    (Kuntner et al. 2025 used the rat cradle with the Quad86 coil); cradleY 19.740 mm
# spres_run.py --bed rat replaces the /control/execute line below and sets cradleY in RUN SETTINGS.
# Every part is placed at (0, {cradleY}, {sourceZ}), so the cradle carries the source along z only
# (z = 0 or 37.5 mm). The radial offsets (+y) move the source alone, upwards, above the cradle; in the
# measurement it sits on a holder, which is not modelled. At 0 mm the bottom face of the cube is
# 0.01 mm above the cradle floor and the side walls are beside it (about 7 mm away for the mouse
# cradle, further for the rat cradle). Moving along +y takes the cube away from the cradle.
# Both cradles stay inside the coil (rat cradle up to r 39.1 mm, coil from 43.0 mm).

/control/execute coil_bed_real/mouse_cradle3mm_cut80_sens.mac



###################### POINT-SOURCE HOLDER ######################
#///////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# NEMA NU 4-2008 Sec. 3.3.3: the point source is embedded in an acrylic cube of 10.0 mm on all
# sides (Kuntner et al. 2025: Na-22, 0.3 mm diameter, 10 mm acrylic cube).
# The material 'Plastic' in Material/GateMaterials.db is C5H8O2 (atom counts) at 1.18 g/cm3, that is
# acrylic (PMMA). I do not use the database entry named 'PMMA': it gives the C5H8O2 atom counts as
# mass fractions (53 % hydrogen by mass instead of 8 %).
# Position = {sourceX} {sourceY} {sourceZ} from RUN SETTINGS. The cradle follows only sourceZ, so
# the source moves radially (along +y) above the cradle. At 0 mm the cube rests on the cradle floor
# (0.01 mm gap); at 35 mm its corners reach 40.3 mm from the scanner axis (coil from 43.0 mm).
# Keep the offsets along +y (see the CRADLE section).

/gate/world/daughters/name                   sourceCube	# free name
/gate/world/daughters/insert                 box		# geometry
/gate/sourceCube/setMaterial                 Plastic		# acrylic (C5H8O2, 1.18 g/cm3)
/gate/sourceCube/geometry/setXLength         10. mm		# NEMA: 10 mm on all sides
/gate/sourceCube/geometry/setYLength         10. mm
/gate/sourceCube/geometry/setZLength         10. mm
/gate/sourceCube/placement/setTranslation    {sourceX} {sourceY} {sourceZ} mm	# set in RUN SETTINGS
/gate/sourceCube/vis/setColor                gray
/gate/sourceCube/vis/setVisible              1
/gate/sourceCube/vis/forceSolid

####################### ATTACH PhantomSD ####################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# PhantomSD records the Compton and Rayleigh scatter in passive volumes (front pad, copper, coil,
# cradle, source cube), so the ROOT output can label scattered coincidences. It does not change the
# physics: without it the scatter still happens, it is only not labelled. Scatter labelled this way
# is not the NEMA scatter fraction, which needs the NEMA projection analysis.

/gate/FrontPad/attachPhantomSD
/gate/shielding/attachPhantomSD

# The coil tubes and the cradle parts are attached to PhantomSD in their own macros in coil_bed_real/.

/gate/sourceCube/attachPhantomSD



########################### PHYSICS #########################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# Physics list for the interactions of the positrons and photons with the materials of the model.


/gate/physics/addPhysicsList empenelope

/gate/physics/addProcess RadioactiveDecay
/gate/physics/addAtomDeexcitation
# empenelope already includes positron annihilation, so I do not add the separate GATE
# PositronAnnihilation process (that would annihilate twice).
# With this GATE 8.2 / Geant4 10.5 Penelope model, annihilation at rest gives exactly opposite
# photons. The decay, the positron spectrum and the positron range and transport are simulated,
# but the photon acollinearity is not: spres_analysis.py adds it.
#/gate/physics/addProcess PositronAnnihilation

# Production cut of 0.1 mm in the world volume. A production cut limits the creation of secondaries; it does
# not stop the primary positron. Daughter volumes take the cuts of their region.
/gate/physics/Gamma/SetCutInRegion          world 0.1 mm
/gate/physics/Electron/SetCutInRegion       world 0.1 mm
/gate/physics/Positron/SetCutInRegion       world 0.1 mm
# Production cut of 0.01 mm in the acrylic source cube (the phantom of this test).
/gate/physics/Gamma/SetCutInRegion          sourceCube   0.01 mm
/gate/physics/Electron/SetCutInRegion       sourceCube   0.01 mm
/gate/physics/Positron/SetCutInRegion       sourceCube   0.01 mm

# The step length of positrons in the acrylic source cube (the phantom of this test) is limited to 0.05 mm, so that their curved paths in the
# 9.4 T magnetic field are followed accurately.
/gate/physics/ActivateStepLimiter          e+
/gate/physics/SetMaxStepSizeInRegion        sourceCube   0.05 mm




####################### INITIALIZATION ######################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# All volumes must be defined before the initialization. The radioactive sources are defined after
# it (the cube that holds the source is part of the geometry, so it comes before).

/gate/run/initialize

# Geant4 overlap check of the geometry before the events are simulated. It is a sampled check:
# shared surfaces are intended, overlaps are not.
/geometry/test/verbosity true
/geometry/test/resolution 10000
/geometry/test/tolerance 0. mm
/geometry/test/run



########################## SOURCE ##########################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\

# NEMA NU 4-2008 Sec. 3.3.2/3.3.3 and Kuntner et al. 2025: Na-22 point source, 0.3 mm diameter,
# embedded in a 10 mm acrylic cube (sourceCube above), 140 kBq, 60 s per position.
# Na-22 ions at rest followed by radioactive decay: GATE/Geant4 produce the beta+ (90 %) and EC
# branches, the positron spectrum, the positron transport in the 9.4 T field and the 1274.5 keV
# gamma. The activity is in nuclear decays per second, not photon pairs per second, so it must not
# be multiplied by the positron branching ratio. The half-life is fixed at 2.6019 y; the decay
# during 60 s is negligible.
# The source is attached to sourceCube, so its position follows {sourceX} {sourceY} {sourceZ}.

/gate/source/addSource Na22PointSource
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setActivity {activity} becquerel	# set in RUN SETTINGS (140 kBq)

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setType            	gps
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/particle       	ion
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/ion       		11 22 0 0	# Z, A, charge, excitation energy
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/monoenergy     	0. eV	# parent ion at rest; this is not the positron energy
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setForcedUnstableFlag 	true
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/setForcedHalfLife 	82056672 s	# 2.6019 y

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/type 		Volume
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/shape 		Sphere
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/radius         0.15 mm	# 0.3 mm diameter (NEMA: <= 0.3 mm in all directions)
/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/centre 		0. 0. 0. mm	# centre of sourceCube

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/gps/angtype 		iso

/gate/source/Na22PointSource/attachTo           sourceCube	# coordinates local to the acrylic cube

# --- Ga-68 alternative (commented out) ----------------------------------------------------------
# Same point, cube and activity alias, Ga-68 instead of Na-22. Do not uncomment it by hand while
# the Na-22 source above is active (that would put two sources in the cube). Instead run
#   python3 spres_run.py --isotope Ga68
# which comments out every active Na22PointSource line and removes the "#GA68 " prefixes below.
# Ga-68 ions at rest followed by radioactive decay: beta+ (about 89 %) and EC, the Ga-68 positron
# spectrum (end point 1.9 MeV, Na-22: 0.55 MeV, so a much longer positron range and a wider point
# image) and the 1077 keV gamma (about 3 %). The half-life comes from the Geant4 decay data
# (about 67.7 min) with useDefaultHalfLife, as for Lu-176: {activity} is the activity at the start
# and falls by about 1 % during 60 s.
# Ga-68 is not the NEMA source, and Kuntner et al. 2025 measured Na-22. Compare the Ga-68 widths
# with the Na-22 simulation (spres_analysis.py --compare-with), not with the measurement.
# Positron range in water: mean about 2.2-3.5 mm (values differ between publications), maximum
# 8-9 mm; acrylic stops positrons slightly better. Nearly all annihilate inside the cube (5 mm to
# every face).
# At 9.4 T a positron stays within 0.84 mm (gyroradius at 1.9 MeV) of its field line across the
# field, so the range widens the image mainly along z (axial width) and no positron reaches the
# side faces. Only a small fraction from the top of the spectrum, emitted roughly along z, gets
# through the +-z faces; in air it travels far beyond the FOV, so it is a lost count, not a wider
# image.

#GA68 /gate/source/addSource Ga68PointSource
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/setActivity {activity} becquerel	# set in RUN SETTINGS

#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/setType            	gps
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/particle       	ion
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/ion       		31 68 0 0	# Z, A, charge, excitation energy.
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/monoenergy     	0. eV	# Stationary parent ion; this is not the positron energy.
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/setForcedUnstableFlag 	true
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/useDefaultHalfLife

#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/type 		Volume
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/shape 		Sphere
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/radius         0.15 mm	# same 0.3 mm diameter point as Na-22
#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/centre 		0. 0. 0. mm	# centre of sourceCube

#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/gps/angtype 		iso

#GA68 /gate/source/Ga68PointSource/attachTo           sourceCube	# coordinates local to the acrylic cube

# The point source (Na-22, or Ga-68 with --isotope Ga68) is defined first, so it gets sourceID 0
# and Lu-176 gets sourceID 1 (spres_analysis.py --source-id 0 finds the point with its events).



########################## Intrinsic Activity Source ##########################
#////////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\
# Intrinsic Lu-176 activity of the LYSO crystals. All results in the paper were run with this
# source switched off: spres_run.py --no-lu176 comments out its lines in the job macros.

/gate/source/addSource Lu176CrystalSource
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/setActivity 152000 becquerel

/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/setType gps
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/particle ion
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/ion 71 176 0 0
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/monoenergy 0. eV
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/setForcedUnstableFlag true
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/useDefaultHalfLife

# The source is sampled in world coordinates from a cylinder around the detector, and the
# confinement keeps only points inside the LYSO crystals. All repeated crystal placements are named
# crystal_phys, so this one source covers all eight heads and all three rings. The cylinder is only
# a sampling region: it adds no material. Do not attach this source to a single crystal.

/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/type Volume
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/shape Cylinder
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/radius 75. mm
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/halfz 78. mm	# encloses the crystals up to +-77 mm (52 mm ring pitch)
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/centre 0. 0. 0. mm
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/angtype iso
/gate/source/Lu176CrystalSource/gps/confine crystal_phys



####################### VISUALIZATION ######################
#/////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The viewer commands are commented out for the headless GATE 8.2 Docker image. To look at the
# model, view_model.py writes a separate view macro.

#/vis/open 						OGLI		# to look at the model, use view_model.py instead
#/vis/viewer/zoom 					18		# zoom in/out
#/vis/viewer/set/viewpointThetaPhi 	60 60	# rotate
#/vis/drawVolume                        
#/vis/viewer/update
#/gate/geometry/rebuild



######################## DIGITIZER #########################
#/////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# The digitizer simulates the detector electronics. It is a chain of signal processors; the output
# of each step is a pulse, and the pulses at the end of the chain are the singles, the detector
# response to the particles. The digitizer turns the hits into singles.
# Order: the adder before the readout; the readout and the energy blurring before the thresholder
# and upholder.


### ADDER ####
# The first step sums all hits in the same crystal (the same volume) into one pulse.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert adder


### READOUT ###
# The readout combines the pulses of one group, set by the depth, into one pulse.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 		  	readout
/gate/digitizer/Singles/readout/setPolicy    TakeEnergyWinner	# keeps the energy-weighted position within each monolithic crystal
/gate/digitizer/Singles/readout/setDepth  	3 

# Depth 3 is the crystal in this PETscanner hierarchy: 24 monolithic blocks. The adder position is
# the energy-weighted centroid of the interactions, and this readout keeps it. SiPM light sharing,
# detector position errors, DOI estimation and a 0.48 mm pixel grid are not simulated here;
# spres_analysis.py adds measured position errors before the reconstruction.


### ENERGY RESOLUTION ###
# Only one energy blurring stage is used: crystalblurring below. The generic blurring module stays
# commented out, because both together would blur the energy twice.

#/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				    		blurring
#/gate/digitizer/Singles/blurring/setResolution	    		0.15
#/gate/digitizer/Singles/blurring/setEnergyOfReference 	511. keV


### CRYSTAL RESOLUTION ###
# Spatial resolution: a fixed energy resolution of 15.2 % FWHM at 511 keV (the count-rate and
# sensitivity models use 18 %). This is the energy resolution this scanner measured at the activity
# of the Na-22 point source: Bruker per-module mean 15.2 % (modules 14.2-16.5 %) at 0.14 MBq in the
# 2022-09-15 Quad35 data, and 14.7 % in the Jan 2024 sensitivity summaries (92 kBq). The 18 % of the
# count-rate model is the value at 25.7 MBq (17.9 %); the resolution gets worse with the count rate.
# GATE 8.2 scales the absolute width with sqrt(E). Equal Min and Max keep one fixed resolution
# instead of drawing a new one for every pulse. The QE of 0.90 is an assumption.
# In this module QE is a per-pulse acceptance: rejected pulses get zero energy. It is not the photon
# detection efficiency of the SiPM. No optical photons are transported.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert							   	crystalblurring
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalResolutionMin 	   	0.152
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalResolutionMax 		0.152
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalQE 			   	0.90
/gate/digitizer/Singles/crystalblurring/setCrystalEnergyOfReference 	511. keV


# Remove the zero-energy pulses rejected by the QE before they enter the electronics, so they cannot
# cause false pile-up. The 1 eV threshold only removes these; it is not the energy window.
/gate/digitizer/Singles/name                    zeroEnergyFilter
/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert                  thresholder
/gate/digitizer/Singles/zeroEnergyFilter/setThreshold 0.001 keV


### PILE-UP ###
# Pile-up window of 100 ns per monolithic block (depth 3). It is an empirical electronics parameter,
# not only the LYSO decay time. Pulses that the final energy window rejects can still cause pile-up
# and deadtime. GATE's pile-up sums the energies and keeps the position and event label of one
# pulse, so the event labels cannot identify every mixed event.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert		 pileup
/gate/digitizer/Singles/pileup/setDepth  3
/gate/digitizer/Singles/pileup/setPileup 100 ns


### DEAD-TIME ###
# Two deadtime stages for each of the 24 crystals: 200 ns paralysable followed by 600 ns
# nonparalysable. They act one after the other; they are not one 800 ns deadtime. Both stages come
# before the final energy window.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				deadtime
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime/setDeadTime 	200. ns
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime/setMode 		paralysable
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime/chooseDTVolume	crystal

/gate/digitizer/Singles/name 					deadtime2
/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				deadtime
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime2/setDeadTime 	600. ns
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime2/setMode 		nonparalysable
/gate/digitizer/Singles/deadtime2/chooseDTVolume 	crystal


### THRESHOLDER ###
# Spatial resolution: 30 % energy window, 357.7-664.3 keV, as in Kuntner et al. 2025 (spatial
# resolution methods). The count-rate model uses 255.55-766.5 keV.

/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				thresholder
/gate/digitizer/Singles/thresholder/setThreshold  357.7 keV
/gate/digitizer/Singles/insert 				upholder
/gate/digitizer/Singles/upholder/setUphold 		664.3 keV


### COINCIDENCE SORTER ###
# GATE opens the window [t_first, t_first + W) at the first single of a prompt coincidence, so
# W = 7 ns accepts pairs with |t1 - t2| < 7 ns. 7 ns is the coincidence window of the measurement
# (Kuntner et al. 2025). No timing blur is added, because no measured single-channel timing
# resolution is available.

/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setInputName       Singles
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setDepth           1
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/minSectorDifference 3
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/allPulseOpenCoincGate false
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setPresortBufferSize 256

/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/setWindow            7 ns	# pairs with |t1 - t2| < 7 ns, see above
/gate/digitizer/Coincidences/MultiplesPolicy    takeAllGoods	# gave the best match with the measurement
# (killAll gave fewer randoms; takeWinnerIfIsGood gave more trues and randoms and slightly different
# scatter.)

/gate/digitizer/name                            delay
/gate/digitizer/insert                          coincidenceSorter
# Delayed coincidence window for the randoms estimate, 10 microseconds after the prompt window:
# far outside the 600 ns deadtime, so the delayed window is not correlated with the deadtime of the
# prompt partner.
/gate/digitizer/delay/setOffset                 10000. ns
/gate/digitizer/delay/setWindow                 7 ns				# same as the prompt window
/gate/digitizer/delay/setInputName              Singles
/gate/digitizer/delay/setDepth                  1
/gate/digitizer/delay/minSectorDifference       3
/gate/digitizer/delay/allPulseOpenCoincGate     false
/gate/digitizer/delay/setPresortBufferSize      256
/gate/digitizer/delay/MultiplesPolicy           takeAllGoods

# The prompt and the delayed sorter use the same multiples policy. GATE's delayed sorter only pairs
# with the seed pulse, so at high multiplicity it is not a perfect randoms estimate.
# The GATE 8.2 sorter does not empty its last presort buffer at the end of the run, so very short
# runs lose a few events at the end; they are not count-rate tests.



######################### OUTPUT ###########################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#
# ROOT output. The file name, with its folder, is set in RUN SETTINGS.

/gate/output/root/enable
/gate/output/root/setFileName {output}				# set in RUN SETTINGS, e.g. output/SpRes_mouse_z0_r10
/gate/output/root/setRootHitFlag 			0		# 0 = do not store the hits, 1 = store them
/gate/output/root/setRootSinglesFlag 		0		# 0 = do not store the singles, 1 = store them
/gate/output/root/setRootCoincidencesFlag 	1		# always 1: the analysis reads the coincidences
/gate/output/root/setRootdelayFlag 		1		# always 1: delayed coincidences



################ RANDOM NUMBER GENERATOR ###################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

# Engines: JamesRandom, Ranlux64, MersenneTwister
/gate/random/setEngineName JamesRandom
/gate/random/setEngineSeed {seed}	# set in RUN SETTINGS; different for every position
/gate/random/verbose 1

#/gate/random/setEngineSeed default
#/gate/random/resetEngineFrom fileName
#/gate/random/setEngineSeed default
#/gate/random/setEngineSeed auto



#################### SIMULATION TIME #######################
#////////////////////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\#

/gate/application/setTimeSlice {duration} s	# one time slice for the whole acquisition
/gate/application/setTimeStart 0.0 s
/gate/application/setTimeStop  {duration} s

# Kuntner et al. 2025: 60 s per position at 140 kBq (about 8.4 million Na-22 decays).
# The duration is set in RUN SETTINGS at the top of this file (spres_run.py changes it).
# Every position needs its own random seed: the same seed with the same settings gives the same
# events.



####################
# Start simulation #
####################

/gate/application/startDAQ

#
